"""### Small Laplacian rereferencing

In order to do a Laplacian 4 rereferencing, we need to manually specify which are the 4 closest neighbours for our channels of interest (C3, C4, Cz). For doing so, let's revisit the sensors' positions plot.

![Captura de pantalla de 2023-09-27 14-15-16.png](data:image/png;base64,)

By inspecting the channel cluters we see that for C3 the four closest channels are FC3, C5, C1 and CP3. Similarly, for Cz we have FCz, C1, C2 and CPz, while for C4 the Laplacian cluster is FC4, C6, C2 and CP4.
"""

# Laplacian 4
# Define neighbours dictionary
lap4_dict = dict(C3 = ['FC3', 'C1', 'CP3', 'C5'],
                 Cz = ['FCz', 'C2', 'CPz', 'C1'],
                 C4 = ['FC4', 'C6', 'CP4', 'C2'])
# Get all epochs as a 3D array (Ntrials*Nchannels*Nsamples)
epochs_lap4_array = epochs.copy().get_data()
# Get measurement info (necessary to create an MNE Epochs object)
info = epochs.info

for _, channel in enumerate(['C3', 'C4', 'Cz']):
  channel_idx = info.ch_names.index(channel)
  # Calculate the mean value across the 4 neighbours for each time sample
  lap4_mean = np.mean(epochs.get_data(picks=lap4_dict[channel]), axis=1)
  # Substract the corresponding mean value for each time sample
  epochs_lap4_array[:, channel_idx, :] -= lap4_mean

# Create again an MNE Epochs object for rereferenced data
epochs_lap4 = mne.EpochsArray(epochs_lap4_array, info=info, events=events,
                              event_id=event_id)

right_spectrum_lap4 = epochs_lap4["right"].compute_psd()
left_spectrum_lap4 = epochs_lap4["left"].compute_psd()

fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=[16, 4.8])
plot_PSD(right_spectrum, channels_list, colors_list,
         title='Right hand MI - Original', axes=axs[0], ylim=(0, 60))
plot_PSD(right_spectrum_lap4, channels_list, colors_list,
         title='Right hand MI - Laplacian 4', axes=axs[1], ylim=(0, 60))

fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=[16, 4.8])
plot_PSD(left_spectrum, channels_list, colors_list,
         title='Left hand MI - Original', axes=axs[0], ylim=(0, 60))
plot_PSD(left_spectrum_lap4, channels_list, colors_list,
         title='Left hand MI - Laplacian 4', axes=axs[1], ylim=(0, 60))

"""😲 OMG! What had happened with the spectrums? Have you realized which electrodes have change the more?

---

Let's have a look at the topomaps 👇
"""

fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=[12, 1.2])
right_spectrum.plot_topomap(ch_type="eeg", agg_fun=np.median, cmap='RdBu_r',
                           normalize=True, axes=axs);
fig.suptitle('Right hand MI - Original', y=1.35)
fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=[12, 1.2])
right_spectrum_lap4.plot_topomap(ch_type="eeg", agg_fun=np.median, cmap='RdBu_r',
                               normalize=True, axes=axs);
fig.suptitle('Right hand MI - Laplacian 4', y=1.35)

fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=[12, 1.2])
left_spectrum.plot_topomap(ch_type="eeg", agg_fun=np.median, cmap='RdBu_r',
                           normalize=True, axes=axs);
fig.suptitle('Left hand MI - Original', y=1.35)
fig, axs = plt.subplots(1, 5, figsize=[12, 1.2])
left_spectrum_lap4.plot_topomap(ch_type="eeg", agg_fun=np.median, cmap='RdBu_r',
                               normalize=True, axes=axs);
fig.suptitle('Left hand MI - Laplacian 4', y=1.35)

"""❓ What are those wholes in the topography plots? Do you think it make sense to plot the scalp topography when applying Laplacian to only a few electrodes?!

🤓 Put your answers here...

# Spatio Spectra Decomposition (SSD)

The spatial spectra decomposition method (SSD) proposed by Nikulin et al. [2] allows for the enhacement of the signal-to-noise-ratio (SNR) in a specific frequency band of interest.

Here, we are interest in improving the signal content at the alpha band (8 a 12 Hz), resulting that everything that is beyond this band would be consider the *noise* band.

For doing so, we will use MNE implementation of [SSD](https://mne.tools/dev/generated/mne.decoding.SSD.html) method.
"""

# we import the MNE function
from mne.decoding import SSD
# inspect the function a little bit:
help(SSD)

# define the freq and noise band. Here we take a delta freq of 1 Hz
freqs_sig = 8, 12
freqs_noise = 7, 13

# define the SSD object -
ssd = SSD(
    info=raw.info,
    reg="oas", # to improve cov matrix estimate robustness
    n_components = None, # when None, no dim reduction
    sort_by_spectral_ratio=True,
    filt_params_signal=dict(
        l_freq=freqs_sig[0],
        h_freq=freqs_sig[1],
        l_trans_bandwidth=1,
        h_trans_bandwidth=1,
    ),
    filt_params_noise=dict(
        l_freq=freqs_noise[0],
        h_freq=freqs_noise[1],
        l_trans_bandwidth=1,
        h_trans_bandwidth=1,
    ),
)

# fit the SSD object
ssd.fit(X=raw.get_data())

"""The SSD method sorts the components (eigenvectors) with respect to the eigenvalues values. By default, for methods based on [GEVD](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2104.12356.pdf), the order of the components is related to the descending order of the eigenvalues. But for SSD the authors in [2] proposed to sort the components accordingly to the power spectra information. This is why when we defined our `ssd` object we used `sort_by_spectral_ratio=True`. Let's inspect the eigenvalues 👀:"""

# plotting the eigenvalues
eigenval = ssd.eigvals_ # since we fit ssd object, the eigvals_ param exists

fig, ax = plt.subplots(1)
ax.plot(eigenval, color="orange", label="sorted eigenvalues");
ax.set_xlabel("Eigenvalue Index")
ax.set_ylabel(r"Spectral Ratio $\frac{P_f}{P_{sf}}$")
ax.legend();

"""❓ So..how many ssd components seem to contribute the most to enhacing the SNR in the alpha band?

✋ Maybe 5 are enough? Let's check it out 👇
"""

# transform signal from the sensor space to the ssd (components) space
ssd_sources = ssd.transform(X=raw.get_data())
# since n_components was set to None, which is the size of ssd_sources?
print(np.shape(ssd_sources))

"""These are the sensor signal mapped into the ssd space. Now, we need to check the power spectrum to see if SSD has done it work properly."""

# psd of the mapped signal
import matplotlib as mpl
colors_ = mpl.colormaps['viridis'].resampled(10)

psd, freqs = mne.time_frequency.psd_array_welch(
    ssd_sources, sfreq=raw.info["sfreq"])

below50 = freqs < 50
# highlight the freq. band of interest
bandfilt = (freqs_sig[0] <= freqs) & (freqs <= freqs_sig[1])
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1)

# for the first 10 components
for i in range(10):
  ax.loglog(freqs[below50], psd[i, below50], color=colors_.colors[i,:],
            alpha=0.7, label=["Component #" +  str(i)])

ax.fill_between(freqs[bandfilt], 0, 10000, color="green", alpha=0.15);
ax.set_xlabel("log(frequency)");
ax.set_ylabel("log(power)");
ax.legend();

"""It seems the first 3 components capture the most power spectrum. Let's compare the spectra of the first 3 components with the last 3 components:

"""

# for the first 3 and the last 3
fig, ax = plt.subplots(1)
compo_num = range(len(psd))
for i in range(3):
  ax.loglog(freqs[below50], psd[i, below50], color=colors_.colors[i,:],
            alpha=0.7, label=["Component #" +  str(compo_num[i])])

for i in range(1,4):
  ax.loglog(freqs[below50], psd[-i, below50],
            alpha=0.7, label=["Component #" +  str(compo_num[-i])])

ax.fill_between(freqs[bandfilt], 0, 10000, color="green", alpha=0.15)
ax.set_xlabel("log(frequency)")
ax.set_ylabel("log(power)")
ax.legend();

"""💅 Beautiful!

👀 We can also check how the correspoding SSD patterns looks like. For illustration purposes we will display the topomaps of the first 3 patterns, but you are free to check it for the others!
"""

import copy as cp

aux_info = cp.deepcopy(raw.info)
with aux_info._unlock():
  # setting sfreq = 1 so as to have component per time point
   aux_info["sfreq"] = 1.0
# create an evoked
patterns = mne.EvokedArray(ssd.patterns_.T, aux_info, tmin=0)
# plot the first 3 components
components = np.arange(3)
patterns.plot_topomap(
            times=components,
            time_format=""
);

